<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mu'in al-Din Unur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1149">1149</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Unur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mu'in al-Din Unur was a Turkish mamluk commander who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> as atabeg for the last Burid rulers from about 1138 until his death in 1149. To resist Zengi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> he allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, yet the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a> besieged Damascus in July 1148 and withdrew after five days. His daughter married Zengi's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Unur began as a mamluk, a slave soldier, in the army of Toghtekin, the founder of the Burid dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. When Zengi, the atabeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, besieged Damascus in 1135, he commanded the army defending the city. The Burid Shihab ad-Din Mahmud then sent him to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, with Yusuf ibn Firuz as his lieutenant, and he still held it when Zengi besieged the city briefly in 1137. In 1138 Shihab ad-Din made him atabeg of Damascus with the title Isfahsalar, but when Zengi received Homs in a marriage settlement later that year, Unur was given the castle of Barin instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Shihab ad-Din's assassination on 22 June 1139, Unur governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalbek">Baalbek</a> for the new emir, Jamal ad-Din, and defended it until Zengi took the city with 14 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catapult">catapults</a>. From 1140 he ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> as regent for Jamal ad-Din's son Mujir ad-Din Abaq. That year he besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banias">Banias</a> with King Fulk of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and Prince Raymond of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and handed it to Fulk, and on a visit to Jerusalem with the future chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usama-ibn-munqidh">Usamah ibn Munqidh</a> he negotiated a fuller alliance against Zengi.</p><p>After Zengi was assassinated in 1146, Unur took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalbek">Baalbek</a> from Najm ad-Din Ayyub, father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, and asserted control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a>. In 1147 he allied with Nur ad-Din, and when Altuntash, governor of Bosra and Salkhad, joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> against him, the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> forced the crusaders to withdraw. In August 1147 the caliph Al-Muqtafi, the Seljuk sultan Mas'ud and the Fatimid caliph al-Hafiz recognised him as governor. Against the crusaders in 1148 he reluctantly called on Nur ad-Din and Saif ad-Din Ghazi I, and he may have bribed the besiegers to leave.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Afterwards he had to acknowledge Nur ad-Din as his overlord. In 1149 he raided crusader lands, agreed a two-year truce with King Baldwin III, and patrolled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hauran">Hauran</a> while Nur ad-Din defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> at the Battle of Inab. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in July he fell ill with dysentery; he died on 28 August 1149 and was buried in the university he had founded in the city. Both Ibn al-Qalanisi and the Christian chronicler William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> praised him, William calling him &quot;a man of much wisdom and a lover of our people.&quot; By 1154 Nur ad-Din held Damascus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mu'in ad-Din Unur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>To resist Zengi of Aleppo, the regent <b>Mu'in al-Din Unur</b> allied with Jerusalem, but the Second Crusade nonetheless besieged Damascus in July 1148 and withdrew after five days.</blockquote><a href="/state/burid-emirate-of-damascus">Burid Emirate of Damascus</a></li></ul></section>
